module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Submodule
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulResolutionOfIdealSheaf

/-!
# The quotient computed by a Koszul complex is the quotient by the ideal sheaf

Let `X` be a ringed space, let `J ⊆ O_X` be a sheaf of ideals and let `f₁, …, f_r` be global
sections of `O_X` which generate `J`. The Koszul complex of the morphism `φ : (O_X)ʳ ⟶ O_X`
they define has degree-zero homology the cokernel of `φ`, whereas the quotient of the structure
sheaf by the ideal sheaf is the cokernel of the inclusion `J ↪ O_X`. This file identifies the
two:

`coker(φ) ≅ O_X/J`.

A morphism and the inclusion of its image have the same cokernel, and the hypothesis that the
`fᵢ` generate `J` says exactly that the image of `φ` is `J`. Rather than constructing the image,
the identification is obtained from the two vanishing statements which characterise it: the
composite `(O_X)ʳ ⟶ O_X ↠ O_X/J` is zero because each `fᵢ` is a section of `J`, and the
composite `J ↪ O_X ↠ coker(φ)` is zero because every section of `J` is, near every point of its
domain, an honest `O_X`-combination of the `fᵢ`. Each vanishing produces a map between the two
cokernels by the universal property, and the two maps are mutually inverse because the quotient
maps are epimorphisms.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sectionsHom`: the morphism `(O_X)ʳ ⟶ O_X` of
  sheaves of modules attached to `r` global sections, in the spelling of the structure sheaf of
  a ringed space.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.cokernelSectionsHomIso` and
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.koszulQuotientIsoIdealQuotient`: **the cokernel of
  that morphism is the quotient of the structure sheaf by the ideal sheaf the sections
  generate.**

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.exists_restrict_eq_sum_mul`: **a section of the
  ideal sheaf generated by `f₁, …, f_r` over an open set is, near every point of that open set,
  an `O_X`-linear combination of the `fᵢ`.** This is the local form of generation, obtained from
  the germwise description by lifting the coefficients of a germwise combination to sections on
  a neighbourhood.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sectionsHom_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero` and
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.ι_comp_cokernelπ_sectionsHom_eq_zero`: the two
  vanishing statements.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.freeHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_zero`: a morphism out of a free sheaf of
  modules is zero as soon as the sections naming it are killed, which is what reduces the first
  vanishing to a statement about the `fᵢ` alone.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.germ_val_app_eq_zero_of_restrict`: a section in the image of a morphism
  of sheaves of modules has vanishing germ at a point once the morphism kills a restriction of
  the source section near that point, which is what turns the local form of generation into the
  second vanishing.

## Implementation notes

The free sheaf of modules on `r` generators is `SheafOfModules.free (ULift (Fin r))`, a
sheafified coproduct of copies of the structure sheaf; a local section of it is only *locally* a
combination of the tautological generators, so nothing is computed by unfolding it. Every
statement about the morphism out of it is therefore reduced to the tautological generators,
either through the universal property of the coproduct (`SheafOfModules.isColimitFreeCofan`) or
through the inclusions `SheafOfModules.ιFree`, whose composites with the morphism are given by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ιFree_comp_freeHomEquiv_symm`.

The two cokernels live in the category of modules over the structure sheaf of rings, which has
two spellings: `(sheafCompose J (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)).obj X.sheaf`, used by the ideal
sheaf, and the reducible `toRingCat J X.sheaf`, used by the Koszul development. They are the
same sheaf of rings, but only the second has a presheaf which is syntactically a composite, and
instance synthesis distinguishes them. The work below is therefore done in the first spelling,
through `sectionsHom`, which is the Koszul morphism of
`Stacks.RegularSequence.koszulHomOfSections` written there; the two agree definitionally, which
is `sectionsHom_eq_koszulHomOfSections`.

The local form of generation is proved from the germwise description
`mem_generatedIdealOn_iff`: the germ of the section is a finite combination of the germs of the
`fᵢ`, the coefficients of that combination are germs of sections on a common neighbourhood, and
two sections with the same germ at a point agree on a smaller neighbourhood.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0622](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0622)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 063D](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063D)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Sheaf Opposite TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry
open Stacks.RingedSpace

universe u v₁ u₁

namespace Stacks.SheafOfModules

variable {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C} {R : Sheaf J RingCat.{u}}

/-- The morphism `unit R ⟶ M` named by a section `σ` of `M` sends a section `a` of the structure
sheaf to `a • σ`. -/
theorem unitHomEquiv_symm_val_app {M : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} R} (σ : M.sections) (Y : Cᵒᵖ)
    (a : R.obj.obj Y) :
    (M.unitHomEquiv.symm σ).val.app Y a = a • σ.eval Y := rfl

/-- The morphism named by a section of `M` dies against a morphism out of `M` which kills that
section over every object. -/
theorem unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_zero {M N : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} R} (σ : M.sections)
    (g : M ⟶ N) (h : ∀ Y : Cᵒᵖ, g.val.app Y (σ.eval Y) = 0) :
    M.unitHomEquiv.symm σ ≫ g = 0 := by
  refine _root_.SheafOfModules.hom_ext
    (PresheafOfModules.hom_ext fun Y => ModuleCat.hom_ext ?_)
  have key : ∀ b : R.obj.obj Y,
      (ConcreteCategory.hom (g.val.app Y)) (((M.unitHomEquiv.symm σ).val.app Y) b) = 0 := by
    intro b
    refine Eq.trans (congrArg (fun z => (ConcreteCategory.hom (g.val.app Y)) z)
      (unitHomEquiv_symm_val_app σ Y b)) ?_
    rw [map_smul, h Y, smul_zero]
  exact LinearMap.ext fun a => key a

section Free

variable [HasWeakSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{u}] [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]

/-- The morphism out of a free sheaf of modules named by a family of sections sends `a` times
the `i`-th tautological generator to `a` times the `i`-th section. -/
theorem freeHomEquiv_symm_val_app_ιFree {M : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} R} {I : Type u}
    (s : I → M.sections) (i : I) (Y : Cᵒᵖ) (a : R.obj.obj Y) :
    (M.freeHomEquiv.symm s).val.app Y ((_root_.SheafOfModules.ιFree i).val.app Y a)
      = a • (s i).eval Y :=
  (congrArg (fun g => (_root_.SheafOfModules.Hom.val g).app Y a)
    (ιFree_comp_freeHomEquiv_symm s i)).trans (unitHomEquiv_symm_val_app (s i) Y a)

/-- **A morphism out of a free sheaf of modules is zero as soon as the sections naming it are
killed.** A free sheaf of modules is a coproduct of copies of the structure sheaf, so a morphism
out of it is determined by its composites with the tautological inclusions, and those are the
morphisms named by the individual sections. -/
theorem freeHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_zero {M N : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} R} {I : Type u}
    (s : I → M.sections) (g : M ⟶ N)
    (h : ∀ (i : I) (Y : Cᵒᵖ), g.val.app Y ((s i).eval Y) = 0) :
    M.freeHomEquiv.symm s ≫ g = 0 := by
  refine Cofan.IsColimit.hom_ext (_root_.SheafOfModules.isColimitFreeCofan I) _ _ fun i => ?_
  have h1 : _root_.SheafOfModules.ιFree i ≫ (M.freeHomEquiv.symm s ≫ g) = 0 := by
    rw [← Category.assoc, ιFree_comp_freeHomEquiv_symm, unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_zero _ g (h i)]
  exact h1.trans comp_zero.symm

end Free

/-- A morphism which kills a submodule kills every section of that submodule. -/
theorem val_app_eq_zero_of_mem_submodule {M N : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} R} (K : M.Submodule)
    (g : M ⟶ N) (hg : K.ι ≫ g = 0) (Y : Cᵒᵖ) (u : M.val.obj Y) (hu : u ∈ K.obj Y) :
    g.val.app Y u = 0 := by
  have h : (K.ι ≫ g).val.app Y (⟨u, hu⟩ : K.obj Y) = 0 := by rw [hg]; rfl
  exact h

/-- **A section in the image of a morphism of sheaves of modules on a topological space has
vanishing germ at a point whenever the morphism kills the restriction of the source section to
some neighbourhood of that point.** The germ is computed on that neighbourhood, where the
morphism commutes with restriction. -/
theorem germ_val_app_eq_zero_of_restrict {Z : TopCat.{u}}
    {O : Sheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥Z) RingCat.{u}}
    {A B : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u} O} (g : A ⟶ B) {V W : Opens ↥Z} (hWV : W ≤ V)
    (u : A.val.obj (op V)) (x : ↥Z) (hxW : x ∈ W) (hx : x ∈ V)
    (h : g.val.app (op W) (A.val.map (homOfLE hWV).op u) = 0) :
    TopCat.Presheaf.germ B.val.presheaf V x hx (g.val.app (op V) u) = 0 := by
  have hgerm := TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res_apply B.val.presheaf (homOfLE hWV) x hxW
    (g.val.app (op V) u)
  rw [show B.val.presheaf.map (homOfLE hWV).op (g.val.app (op V) u)
      = g.val.app (op W) (A.val.map (homOfLE hWV).op u) from
    (PresheafOfModules.naturality_apply g.val (homOfLE hWV).op u).symm, h] at hgerm
  exact hgerm.symm.trans (map_zero _)

end Stacks.SheafOfModules

namespace Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf

open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QuasiRegular Stacks.RegularSequence Stacks.SheafOfModules

/-! ### Local generation -/

/-- **Finitely many germs at a point are simultaneously represented on one neighbourhood.** The
neighbourhood is obtained by intersecting one neighbourhood for each index, which is why the
indices are taken from a finite set rather than from the whole index type. -/
theorem exists_common_germ_presheaf {X : TopCat.{u}} (F : TopCat.Presheaf CommRingCat.{u} X)
    (x : X) {ι : Type*} (G : ι → F.stalk x) (t : Finset ι) :
    ∃ (U : Opens X) (hx : x ∈ U) (σ : ι → F.obj (op U)),
      ∀ i ∈ t, F.germ U x hx (σ i) = G i := by
  classical
  induction t using Finset.induction with
  | empty => exact ⟨⊤, trivial, fun _ => 0, by simp⟩
  | insert i t hi ih =>
    obtain ⟨U₁, hx₁, σ₁, hσ₁⟩ := ih
    obtain ⟨U₂, hx₂, s₂, hs₂⟩ := F.exists_germ_eq (G i)
    refine ⟨U₁ ⊓ U₂, ⟨hx₁, hx₂⟩,
      Function.update (fun k => F.map (homOfLE (inf_le_left (a := U₁) (b := U₂))).op (σ₁ k)) i
        (F.map (homOfLE (inf_le_right (a := U₁) (b := U₂))).op s₂), ?_⟩
    intro k hk
    by_cases hki : k = i
    · subst hki
      rw [Function.update_self]
      exact (F.germ_res_apply _ x _ s₂).trans hs₂
    · rw [Function.update_of_ne hki]
      exact (F.germ_res_apply _ x _ (σ₁ k)).trans (hσ₁ k (by simpa [hki] using hk))

/-- Restricting a section twice is restricting it once: the opens of a space form a partial
order, so the two inclusions compose to the third. -/
theorem map_map_apply {X : RingedSpace.{u}} {A B C : Opens X}
    (i : C ⟶ B) (j : B ⟶ A) (k : C ⟶ A) (s : X.presheaf.obj (op A)) :
    X.presheaf.map i.op (X.presheaf.map j.op s) = X.presheaf.map k.op s := by
  rw [← ConcreteCategory.comp_apply, ← X.presheaf.map_comp]
  exact congrArg (fun h => (ConcreteCategory.hom (X.presheaf.map h)) s) (Subsingleton.elim _ _)

/-- **A section of the ideal sheaf generated by `f₁, …, f_r` over `U` is, near every point of
its domain, an `O_X`-linear combination of the `fᵢ`.**

The germ of the section at the point is such a combination of the germs of the `fᵢ`; the
coefficients of that combination are germs of sections on a common neighbourhood; and the
resulting combination of sections has the same germ as the given section, hence agrees with it
on a smaller neighbourhood. -/
theorem exists_restrict_eq_sum_mul {X : RingedSpace.{u}} {U : Opens X} {r : ℕ}
    (f : Fin r → X.presheaf.obj (op U)) (V : Opens X) (hVU : V ≤ U)
    (t : X.presheaf.obj (op V)) (ht : t ∈ (generatedIdealOn X U f).obj (op V))
    (x : X) (hx : x ∈ V) :
    ∃ (W : Opens X) (hWV : W ≤ V) (_ : x ∈ W) (a : Fin r → X.presheaf.obj (op W)),
      X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hWV).op t
        = ∑ i, a i * X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (hWV.trans hVU)).op (f i) := by
  classical
  have hg : X.presheaf.germ V x hx t ∈ Jid (germFamily X U f x (hVU hx)) :=
    (mem_generatedIdealOn_iff U f V hVU t).1 ht x hx
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := (Submodule.mem_span_range_iff_exists_fun _).1 hg
  obtain ⟨W₀, hxW₀, σ, hσ⟩ := exists_common_germ_presheaf X.presheaf x c Finset.univ
  set W₁ : Opens X := W₀ ⊓ V with hW₁
  have hxW₁ : x ∈ W₁ := ⟨hxW₀, hx⟩
  have hW₁V : W₁ ≤ V := inf_le_right
  have hW₁W₀ : W₁ ≤ W₀ := inf_le_left
  set u : X.presheaf.obj (op W₁) :=
    ∑ i, X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hW₁W₀).op (σ i) *
      X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (hW₁V.trans hVU)).op (f i) with hu
  have hgu : X.presheaf.germ W₁ x hxW₁ u = X.presheaf.germ W₁ x hxW₁
      (X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hW₁V).op t) := by
    rw [X.presheaf.germ_res_apply]
    rw [hu, map_sum, ← hc]
    refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
    rw [map_mul, X.presheaf.germ_res_apply, X.presheaf.germ_res_apply,
      hσ i (Finset.mem_univ i), smul_eq_mul]
    rfl
  obtain ⟨W, hxW, iW1, iW2, hW⟩ := X.presheaf.germ_eq x hxW₁ hxW₁ u
    (X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hW₁V).op t) hgu
  refine ⟨W, (leOfHom iW1).trans hW₁V, hxW, fun i => X.presheaf.map iW1.op
    (X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hW₁W₀).op (σ i)), ?_⟩
  rw [← map_map_apply iW2 (homOfLE hW₁V) (homOfLE ((leOfHom iW1).trans hW₁V)) t, ← hW, hu,
    map_sum]
  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
  rw [map_mul]
  congr 1
  exact map_map_apply iW1 (homOfLE (hW₁V.trans hVU)) _ (f i)

/-! ### The morphism attached to a family of global sections -/

variable {X : RingedSpace.{u}} (K : (structureSheafModule X).Submodule) {r : ℕ}
  (f : Fin r → X.presheaf.obj (op (⊤ : Opens X)))

/-- **The morphism `(O_X)ʳ ⟶ O_X` attached to `r` global sections of the structure sheaf**: the
morphism out of the free sheaf of modules on `r` generators sending the `k`-th tautological
generator to `f k`.

It is the morphism whose Koszul complex is the Koszul complex of the sequence `f₁, …, f_r`,
written over the structure sheaf of rings of a ringed space. -/
def sectionsHom :
    _root_.SheafOfModules.free (R := (sheafCompose (Opens.grothendieckTopology X)
      (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).obj X.sheaf) (ULift.{u} (Fin r)) ⟶
      structureSheafModule X :=
  (structureSheafModule X).freeHomEquiv.symm
    fun k => Stacks.PresheafOfModules.sectionOfTop _ (f k.down)

/-- The morphism attached to a family of global sections is the Koszul morphism of that
family. -/
theorem sectionsHom_eq_koszulHomOfSections : sectionsHom f = koszulHomOfSections X.sheaf f := rfl

/-- The value of the morphism attached to `f₁, …, f_r` on a combination of the tautological
generators is the corresponding combination of the `fᵢ`. -/
theorem sectionsHom_val_app_sum (W : Opens X) (a : Fin r → X.presheaf.obj (op W)) :
    (sectionsHom f).val.app (op W)
        (∑ i, (_root_.SheafOfModules.ιFree (ULift.up i)).val.app (op W) (a i))
      = ∑ i, a i * X.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : W ≤ ⊤)).op (f i) := by
  rw [map_sum]
  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
  refine Eq.trans (freeHomEquiv_symm_val_app_ιFree (M := structureSheafModule X)
    (fun k : ULift.{u} (Fin r) =>
      Stacks.PresheafOfModules.sectionOfTop (structureSheafModule X).val (f k.down))
    (ULift.up i) (op W) (a i)) rfl

/-! ### The two vanishing statements -/

/-- **The morphism attached to sections of an ideal sheaf dies in the quotient by that ideal
sheaf.** Each `fᵢ` is a section of the ideal sheaf over the whole space, hence so is each of its
restrictions, and the quotient map kills the ideal sheaf. -/
theorem sectionsHom_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero (hf : ∀ k, f k ∈ K.obj (op (⊤ : Opens X))) :
    sectionsHom f ≫ cokernel.π K.ι = 0 :=
  freeHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_zero _ _ fun k Y =>
    val_app_eq_zero_of_mem_submodule K _ (cokernel.condition K.ι) Y _
      (K.toSubmodule.map_mem (homOfLE (le_top : Y.unop ≤ ⊤)).op (hf k.down))

/-- A section in the image of a morphism of sheaves of modules dies in its cokernel. -/
theorem comp_cokernelπ_val_app_eq_zero {A B : _root_.SheafOfModules.{u}
    ((sheafCompose (Opens.grothendieckTopology X)
      (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})).obj X.sheaf)} (g : A ⟶ B) (Y : (Opens X)ᵒᵖ)
    (z : A.val.obj Y) : (cokernel.π g).val.app Y (g.val.app Y z) = 0 := by
  have h : (g ≫ cokernel.π g).val.app Y z = 0 := by rw [cokernel.condition]; rfl
  exact h

/-- A section of an ideal sheaf generated by `f₁, …, f_r` has vanishing germ in the cokernel of
the morphism attached to the `fᵢ`: near the point it is a combination of the `fᵢ`, hence in the
image of that morphism. -/
theorem germ_cokernelπ_sectionsHom_eq_zero (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f)
    (V : Opens X) (u : X.presheaf.obj (op V)) (hu : u ∈ K.obj (op V)) (x : X) (hx : x ∈ V) :
    TopCat.Presheaf.germ (cokernel (sectionsHom f)).val.presheaf V x hx
      ((cokernel.π (sectionsHom f)).val.app (op V) u) = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨W, hWV, hxW, a, ha⟩ :=
    exists_restrict_eq_sum_mul f V le_top u ((hgen V le_top).le hu) x hx
  have key : (cokernel.π (sectionsHom f)).val.app (op W)
      (X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hWV).op u) = 0 := by
    rw [ha, ← sectionsHom_val_app_sum f W a]
    exact comp_cokernelπ_val_app_eq_zero _ _ _
  exact germ_val_app_eq_zero_of_restrict (cokernel.π (sectionsHom f)) hWV u x hxW hx key

/-- A section of an ideal sheaf generated by `f₁, …, f_r` dies in the cokernel of the morphism
attached to the `fᵢ`: all of its germs vanish, and a section of a sheaf with vanishing germs is
zero. -/
theorem cokernelπ_sectionsHom_val_app_eq_zero (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f)
    (V : Opens X) (u : X.presheaf.obj (op V)) (hu : u ∈ K.obj (op V)) :
    (cokernel.π (sectionsHom f)).val.app (op V) u = 0 :=
  TopCat.Presheaf.section_ext
    (⟨(cokernel (sectionsHom f)).val.presheaf, (cokernel (sectionsHom f)).isSheaf⟩ :
      TopCat.Sheaf AddCommGrpCat.{u} _) V _ 0
    fun x hx => (germ_cokernelπ_sectionsHom_eq_zero K f hgen V u hu x hx).trans (map_zero _).symm

/-- A generating family of an ideal sheaf over the whole space consists of sections of that
ideal sheaf. -/
theorem mem_of_isGeneratedByOn_top (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f) (k : Fin r) :
    f k ∈ K.obj (op (⊤ : Opens X)) :=
  (hgen ⊤ le_rfl).ge (mem_generatedIdealOn X ⊤ f k)

/-- **The inclusion of an ideal sheaf generated by `f₁, …, f_r` dies in the cokernel of the
morphism attached to the `fᵢ`.** -/
theorem ι_comp_cokernelπ_sectionsHom_eq_zero (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f) :
    K.ι ≫ cokernel.π (sectionsHom f) = 0 := by
  refine _root_.SheafOfModules.hom_ext (PresheafOfModules.hom_ext fun Y => ModuleCat.hom_ext
    (LinearMap.ext fun z => ?_))
  obtain ⟨V⟩ := Y
  exact cokernelπ_sectionsHom_val_app_eq_zero K f hgen V z.1 z.2

/-! ### The identification of the two quotients -/

/-- **The cokernel of the morphism attached to a generating family of an ideal sheaf is the
quotient of the structure sheaf by that ideal sheaf.** The two maps are those produced by the
universal property of the two cokernels from the two vanishing statements, and they are mutually
inverse because the quotient maps are epimorphisms. -/
def cokernelSectionsHomIso (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f) :
    cokernel (sectionsHom f) ≅ idealQuotient X K where
  hom := cokernel.desc _ (cokernel.π K.ι)
    (sectionsHom_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero K f (mem_of_isGeneratedByOn_top K f hgen))
  inv := cokernel.desc _ (cokernel.π (sectionsHom f))
    (ι_comp_cokernelπ_sectionsHom_eq_zero K f hgen)
  hom_inv_id := by rw [← cancel_epi (cokernel.π (sectionsHom f))]; simp
  inv_hom_id := by rw [← cancel_epi (cokernel.π K.ι)]; simp

/-- **The quotient computed by the Koszul complex of a generating family of an ideal sheaf is
the quotient of the structure sheaf by that ideal sheaf.** -/
def koszulQuotientIsoIdealQuotient (hgen : IsGeneratedByOn X K ⊤ f) :
    koszulQuotient.{u, u, u} (koszulHomOfSections X.sheaf f) ≅ idealQuotient X K :=
  cokernelSectionsHomIso K f hgen

end Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf

end
